<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Princess Milica of Serbia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1335–1405</span> · Also known as Saint Milica</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Milica was the wife of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar of Serbia</a> and, after his death at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo">Battle of Kosovo</a> in 1389, regent for their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-lazarevic">Stefan Lazarević</a>. She negotiated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> with the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a>, gave her daughter Olivera in marriage to him and kept the principality intact. She later became a nun under the name Eugenia, wrote religious poetry and is venerated as a saint by the Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/princess-milica-of-serbia.jpg" alt="Princess Milica of Serbia" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This image was made by Sadko. Please credit this with: &quot;Sadko, Wikipedia&quot; in the immediate vicinity of the image. A m... · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monument_to_Princess_Milica_of_Serbia_in_Trstenik.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
